-## Contributing
-As long as it's not linux/cross-platform skeleton/compatibility layer, all of the code on the repo that's not behind a preprocessor condition(like FIX_BUGS) are **completely** reversed code from original binaries.  
-
-We **don't** accept custom codes, as long as it's not wrapped via preprocessor conditions, or it's linux/cross-platform skeleton/compatibility layer.
-
-We accept only these kinds of PRs;
-
-- A new feature that exists in at least one of the GTAs (if it wasn't in III/VC then it doesn't have to be decompilation)  
-- Game, UI or UX bug fixes (if it's a fix to original code, it should be behind FIX_BUGS)
-- Platform-specific and/or unused code that's not been reversed yet
-- Makes reversed code more understandable/accurate, as in "which code would produce this assembly".
-- A new cross-platform skeleton/compatibility layer, or improvements to them
-- Translation fixes, for languages original game supported
-- Code that increase maintainability  
-
-We have a [Coding Style](https://github.com/GTAmodding/re3/blob/master/CODING_STYLE.md) document that isn't followed or enforced very well.
+The scripts in [tools/saves](tools/saves) transfer slots between the current
+Win64 PC release and the native Quest release. They preserve the slot number,
+convert the ABI-dependent garages block, validate the checksum and known block
+layout, and back up an existing destination save before overwriting it.
+
+On Windows, connect the Quest over USB, enable USB debugging, close the game,
+then run:
+
+```text
+tools\saves\import-pc-save-to-quest.bat
+tools\saves\export-quest-save-to-pc.bat
+```
 
-Do not use features from C++11 or later.
+Linux and Steam Deck users can run the matching `.sh` scripts. See
+[tools/saves/README.md](tools/saves/README.md) for command-line examples.
 
+Only saves produced by the current Win64 Vice City VR and native Quest builds
+are supported. Original retail saves, old 32-bit/x86 saves, `Vanilla` reVC
+saves, unknown layouts, and corrupt files are deliberately rejected. These
+formats can have a valid size and checksum while containing incompatible C++
+structure layouts. Keep independent backups of important saves.
+
+Version 0.4.0 also fixes the Quest frontend load path so the world is fully
+restarted before a selected save is deserialised.
+
+## Controls and VR menus
+
+- Both grips + Menu: open or close VR settings.
+- Both grips + both triggers + X, or both grips + both triggers + L3: fallback
+  VR-menu shortcut.
+- Both grips + B: open or close the cheat menu.
+- Both grips + A: toggle the debug overlay.
+- Both grips + Y: start or stop a performance capture.
+- B while driving: fire in Default, Immersive, and Motion driving modes.
+
+Inside a VR menu, use the left thumbstick to select, L2/R2 to change a value,
+A to open or confirm, and B to go back.
+
+## Known limitations
+
+- Meta Quest 3 is the primary tested headset. Quest 3S and other devices should
+  be listed as supported only after a hardware test.
+- The complete campaign has not yet been played through on the standalone
+  release configuration.
+- Some missions, vehicles, effects, or controller interactions may still need
+  Quest-specific fixes.
+- Original Windows executable plugins such as ASI and CLEO modules cannot run
+  in the native Android build.
+- Save compatibility is limited to the current Win64 Vice City VR and native
+  Quest formats described above.
+
+## Build from source
+
+The Android application is in `android/`. It builds an `arm64-v8a` APK and
+requires Android SDK 35, NDK `27.2.12479018`, CMake 3.22.1 or newer, JDK 17 or
+newer, and the configured private release key for a release build.
+
+From the `android` directory:
+
+```text
+gradlew :app:assembleDebug
+gradlew :app:assembleRelease
+```
 
-## History
+Release signing is read from `android/release-signing.properties`; the private
+keystore and its passwords must never be committed or included in an archive.
 
-re3 was started sometime in the spring of 2018,
-initially as a way to test reversed collision and physics code
-inside the game.
-This was done by replacing single functions of the game
-with their reversed counterparts using a dll.
+Important source locations:
 
-After a bit of work the project lay dormant for about a year
-and was picked up again and pushed to github in May 2019.
-At the time I (aap) had reversed around 10k lines of code and estimated
-the final game to have around 200-250k.
-Others quickly joined the effort (Fire_Head, shfil, erorcun and Nick007J
-in time order, and Serge a bit later) and we made very quick progress
-throughout the summer of 2019
-after which the pace slowed down a bit.
+- `src/`: reverse-engineered game systems.
+- `src/vr/QuestDrivingVR.cpp`: native vehicle interaction and input.
+- `src/vr/QuestWeaponVR.cpp`: tracked hands, weapons, holsters, and VR menus.
+- `src/skel/android/`: Android platform, storage, audio, and lifecycle code.
+- `vendor/librw/`: RenderWare-compatible renderer and Vulkan backend.
+- `release/sidequest/`: public release documentation and publishing checklist.
+- `tools/saves/`: validated PC/Quest save conversion and transfer tools.
 
-Due to everyone staying home during the start of the Corona pandemic
-everybody had a lot of time to work on re3 again and
-we finally got a standalone exe in April 2020 (around 180k lines by then).
+## Credits
 
-After the initial excitement and fixing and polishing the code further,
-reVC was started in early May 2020 by starting from re3 code,
-not by starting from scratch replacing functions with a dll.
-After a few months of mostly steady progress we considered reVC
-finished in December.
+- re3/reVC contributors for the reverse-engineered game code.
+- librw contributors for the RenderWare-compatible renderer.
+- Khronos Group for OpenXR and Vulkan.
+- VRMADA for the UltimateXR hand assets used under the MIT License.
 
-Since then we have started reLCS, which is currently work in progress.
 
 
